  "query_preview": "(This evening through Wednesday)...\nIssued at 331 PM EST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\nTonight through Thursday night....\n\nStrong upper low over the Mid-MS valley will shift E-SE then east \ninto the Ohio Valley through the period. Ahead of this system, a \nplume of moist air supported by moderately strong isentropic \nlift/WAA will support a broad axis of light to moderate rainfall \nshifting north into the Indiana starting late tonight in southern \nzones and across the remainder of the forecast area early Thursday. \nWidespread 1 to 1.25 inches of rainfall are expected across central \nIndiana. This will be the most widespread rain on Thanksgiving since \n2010 and the most rainfall in one calendar day since August 19, \n2026. Strengthening surface low pressure over the Ohio Valley \ncombined with steep low level lapse rates from the surface to 1 km \nAGL support wind gusts up to 35 mph across areas along I-70 where \nsurface pressure gradient will be maximized during Thursday \nafternoon. \n\nForecast soundings support rain through Thursday, but dynamic \ncooling Thursday night is expected to favor a transition to a mix of \nrain/snow mix and a brief changeover to snow for areas east of I-65. \nSurface temperatures will remain warm and with air temperatures not \nexpected to fall below 32F, little to no snow accumulation is \nexpected across central Indiana. \n\nFriday through Saturday....\n\nGiven the rapid eastward movement of the upper low Friday as a \nstrong mid-upper level jetstreak rotates into the eastern seaboard, \nexpect a fairly quick rebound in WAA development. Early morning \nclouds and possibly light showers will transition to partly cloudy \nskies by afternoon as larger scale subsidence move into central \nIndiana. Fairly strong pressure gradient combined with steep low \nlevel lapse rates will support gusty winds through late Friday \nbefore weakening by evening. Lows Friday night are expected to be in \nthe mid-upper 20s with clear skies and light winds. Highs by \nSaturday will rebound to above normal in the lower to mid 50s under \npartly cloudy skies and moderate SW winds. \n\nSunday and Sunday night...\n\nMedium range guidance has come into better agreement with recent \nEuro runs with a deepening shortwave trough and attendant cold front \nmoving across central Indiana Sunday and Sunday evening. \nPrecipitation should be in the form of all rain given thickness \nvalues remaining above 540 dm. Moderately strong pressure gradient \ncombined with steep low level lapse rates will support another day \nof breezy conditions with gusts up to 25 mph ahead of and behind the \ncold front. \n\nMonday and beyond... \n\nCAA will continue Monday albeit weakening in the wake of the cold \nfront/upper trough. Weakening winds with approaching surface high \npressure will set the stage for the coldest night for the next 7 \ndays on Monday night with lows in the low-mid 20s expected most \nareas. Looking at the larger scale pattern, zonal flow will commence \nMonday with weaker shortwave trough energy expected to move across \nthe region midweek. At this time threat for any precip is low.",
